Late-Diagnosed Neurodivergence Ontological Shock - Adjustment Disorder - V62.89 (Phase of life problem)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Retrospective grief, Life-review compulsion, Unmasking burnout

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-026
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of profound emotional distress and complete cessation of activities of daily living necessitating urgent psychiatric stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a sudden onset of severe, incapacitating grief, total sleep cessation for 48 hours, and a complete withdrawal from all social and occupational responsibilities following a recent diagnostic disclosure. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was required to definitively rule out acute suicidal ideation and prevent further physiological decompensation due to profound self-neglect prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel like my entire life has been a lie, and the exhaustion of realizing how hard I've been pretending has physically broken me.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an intense phase of identity re-evaluation following a recent adult diagnosis of autism/ADHD. Over the past 48 hours, they have experienced alternating waves of profound relief and deep grief for a lifetime of misunderstood struggles. The patient describes a compulsive life-review, recontextualizing past traumas through the new diagnostic lens, leading to total insomnia. There is marked frustration and anger directed at educational and medical systems for missing early signs. A sudden and intense unmasking has led to acute periods of social withdrawal and profound burnout from realizing the extent of energy previously spent mimicking neurotypical behavior. The patient reports a rapid shifting of social circles to seek neuro-affirming groups and a hyper-focus on acquiring specialized knowledge regarding the new diagnosis, neglecting basic hydration and nutrition in the process.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; bedbound and unable to perform basic self-care secondary to profound cognitive and emotional exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration and prolonged occupational decline due to sudden, complete unmasking and burnout.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears disheveled, physically exhausted, exhibiting flattened affect and avoiding eye contact.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild resting tachycardia, normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Clear breath sounds bilaterally. Neurological: Alert and oriented. Cranial nerves intact. No focal motor or sensory deficits. Cognitive processing is noticeably delayed, consistent with severe unmasking burnout. The clinical interview is dominated by a life-review compulsion; the patient repeatedly attempts to analyze childhood events rather than answering acute medical questions. Retrospective grief is palpable, presenting as sudden bouts of tearfulness during the physical examination.
Environmental Observations: The residence is completely dark; multiple books on neurodivergence are open on the floor alongside untouched food.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications. Denies illicit substance use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Late-Diagnosed Neurodivergence Ontological Shock - Adjustment Disorder - V62.89 (Phase of life problem) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Major Depressive Episode - Ruled out/unlikely as the profound sadness is explicitly retrospective and tied directly to the shock of the new diagnostic paradigm, rather than a global, endogenous anhedonia.

Acute Psychosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of hallucinations, delusions, or disorganized thought processes; the hyper-focus and life-review are intensely logical and rooted in reality.

Bipolar I Disorder (Manic Phase) - Ruled out/unlikely despite the decreased need for sleep and hyper-focus, as there is no grandiosity, pressured speech, or reckless behavior; the insomnia is driven by grief and cognitive processing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe psychomotor retardation, resting tachycardia (HR 102), and a 48-hour period of complete insomnia and oral restriction driven by the profound ontological shock of a late neurodivergent diagnosis. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a comprehensive mental state examination, exclude an acute major depressive episode with suicidal intent, and force a break in the insomnia cycle to prevent severe metabolic or psychiatric decompensation. Delaying care until routine hours would have left the patient in an unmonitored state of severe self-neglect and psychological crisis, firmly satisfying the criteria for urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral promethazine 25mg to initiate sedation and break the acute insomnia cycle. Guided oral fluid intake on site. Advised the usual GP to review formal neuropsychological diagnostic confirmation of neurodivergence and seek psychometric evidence of adult-onset identity integration.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the unmasking burnout and safely restore energy, implement a "sensory fasting" protocol: spend 2 hours daily in a completely dark, silent room with zero demands; prioritizing this sensory reduction will physically calm the nervous system and validate the new diagnosis. To control the life-review compulsion, utilize a structured "processing journal": limit writing about past traumas to exactly 20 minutes a day, physically closing the book afterward to prevent the cognitive loop from causing further insomnia.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a neurodiversity-affirming psychologist or occupational therapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 72 hours to monitor sleep restoration, fluid intake, and initiate long-term affirming care pathways.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing the sudden emergence of active suicidal thoughts, an inability to drink fluids for more than 12 hours, or extreme dizziness upon standing.

